{% trace-scenario title="Choose the first fix" prompt="An agent answered confidently, used the wrong tool, and cost twice as much as usual. Which evidence do you inspect first?" outcome="Start with the trace and user-visible outcome, then separate prompt, tool, retrieval, and policy causes before changing the model." /%}

## At a glance

![Four diagnostic lanes converge on a production AI-agent decision: prompt, tool, retrieval, or human review](/media/ai-agent-evaluation-in-production-diagnostic-lanes.png)

If your AI agent is already serving users, offline benchmark scores are no longer enough. The question is not only whether a model can produce a good answer. It is whether the complete system chose an acceptable plan, called the right tools, handled uncertainty, respected permissions, and completed the user’s job at a sustainable cost.

This guide is for engineers and operators who need to make that decision from production evidence. You will learn a framework called **TRACE**: target, record, assess, classify, and evolve. It is a decision framework, not a promise that every agent can be reduced to one score. It deliberately does not cover model training, legal certification, or a vendor-specific evaluator API.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Operating principle" title="A score is a signal; a trace explains the decision." body="Never ship a quality number without retaining enough execution context to understand what produced it, what it cost, and what the user experienced." tone="yellow" /%}

## Why production evaluation is different

An offline test usually starts with a fixed input and checks an output. A production agent has a changing context: user permissions, retrieved documents, tool availability, model routing, retries, timeouts, and side effects. The same prompt can therefore produce materially different executions.

That difference creates a common trap. A team sees a lower answer score and edits the system prompt. The next sample improves, but the actual failure was stale retrieval or a tool returning an incomplete record. Another team sees a high judge score and ignores that the agent made three unnecessary calls and exposed an internal identifier in its explanation. Both teams are optimizing a symptom.

The [AI agent evaluation practical guide](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) is useful for designing datasets and evaluators. This article starts later in the lifecycle: the run has happened, a user or monitor has raised a question, and you need to decide what evidence justifies a change.

## The TRACE operating model

TRACE turns “the agent felt wrong” into a sequence of evidence checks.

| Stage | Question | Minimum evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Target | What does success mean for this task? | User outcome and hard constraints |
| Record | What actually happened? | Trace, inputs, outputs, tools, timing, cost |
| Assess | How good, safe, and complete was it? | Rubric, automated checks, or human label |
| Classify | Where did the first meaningful divergence occur? | Span-level diagnosis and counterfactual |
| Evolve | What change should be tested and released? | Regression set, owner, threshold, rollback |

The order matters. A classification made before the trace is read is usually a guess. An evolution step without a regression set is an uncontrolled experiment in production.

### Define target before score

Write the task contract in observable terms. “Helpful” is too vague. For a support agent, a stronger contract might be: identify the correct account, retrieve the current policy, answer with a supported explanation, avoid unauthorized changes, and ask for a human when confidence is insufficient.

Separate hard constraints from preferences. A fabricated policy citation is a release blocker even if the prose is fluent. A response that takes one extra second may be acceptable if it prevents a wrong account update. {% highlight %}Quality dimensions need an order of precedence{% /highlight %} before they can guide a release.

### Record the whole execution

The useful unit is the run, but the useful diagnosis is usually a span inside that run. Record a stable run identifier, task type, environment, model route, prompt version, retrieval context, tool arguments and results, retries, latency, token usage, estimated cost, final output, and failure state. Redact secrets and minimize personal data before retention.

| Signal | What it can tell you | What it cannot prove alone |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Final answer score | Whether the visible response met a rubric | Which internal step caused the result |
| Tool error rate | Whether integrations are failing | Whether the agent chose the right tool |
| Latency p95 | Whether tail experience is degrading | Whether a faster answer is correct |
| Cost per task | Whether usage is changing | Whether the extra usage bought value |
| Human escalation | Whether uncertainty reaches people | Whether escalation is too high or too low |

## Assess quality without hiding failures

Use several narrow checks instead of one composite score. A task can pass correctness while failing authorization. It can pass relevance while violating a format contract. It can be safe but too slow for the user’s workflow.

A practical evaluation record contains a task label, a rubric version, individual dimensions, evidence, and a disposition. The disposition should be one of pass, review, retry, or fail. Keep the explanation short but concrete: “selected invoice_lookup when the request required subscription_lookup” is more actionable than “agent quality: 0.42.”

### Use judges as reviewers, not authorities

LLM-as-a-judge evaluation can scale review, especially for relevance, completeness, and style. It is weaker when the answer depends on a private fact, exact arithmetic, authorization, or a side effect. For those dimensions, prefer deterministic assertions or human review.

The [LLM-as-a-judge evaluation guide](/blog/llm-as-a-judge-evaluation) explains rubric design in more detail. In production, also sample judge disagreements. A stable high score with frequent user corrections is a monitoring failure, not evidence that users are wrong.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Diagnostic shortcut" title="Find the first divergence, not the loudest symptom." body="Read the trace from the user request forward. Mark the first point where the execution no longer satisfies the task contract. Later errors may be consequences rather than causes." tone="dark" /%}

## Classify the failure by its first divergence

Four categories cover many agent incidents. They are not mutually exclusive, but the first divergence gives you the smallest useful place to start.

| Category | First divergence | Typical evidence | First experiment |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Prompt or policy | The plan violates an explicit instruction | Wrong format, missing constraint, unjustified confidence | Change one instruction and replay |
| Tool | The right intent becomes a wrong call or bad result | Invalid arguments, stale schema, timeout, permission error | Validate schema and tool contract |
| Retrieval or context | The agent reasons from missing or misleading context | Relevant source absent, conflicting passages, truncation | Inspect retrieval and context budget |
| Human review | The task exceeds safe automation bounds | Ambiguous identity, high-impact action, low evidence | Add a clear approval gate |

### Prompt failures

Prompt fixes are appropriate when the trace shows the agent had the required facts and permissions but ignored a clear instruction. Test the smallest change possible. Pin the prompt version, replay a representative set, and look for regressions in refusals, tool selection, verbosity, and cost.

Do not use a prompt to compensate for missing validation. “Always use the exact account ID” does not make an unvalidated account ID safe. The application or tool boundary should enforce constraints that must never be violated.

### Tool failures

Tool problems often masquerade as reasoning failures. A tool can silently return an empty list, change its field names, apply a timezone differently, or accept a syntactically valid but semantically dangerous argument. Instrument tool inputs and outputs with a redacted schema-aware representation, and record whether the result was authoritative, partial, or stale.

Illustrative TypeScript for a narrow tool boundary:

```ts
type ToolResult =
  | { ok: true; source: "authoritative" | "cached"; records: number }
  | { ok: false; kind: "timeout" | "invalid_input" | "permission" };

function mayContinue(result: ToolResult): boolean {
  return result.ok && result.source === "authoritative" && result.records > 0;
}
```

This is illustrative rather than a drop-in Uncoil SDK call. The important behavior is explicit: a cached or empty result is not silently treated as proof. Add tests for malformed arguments, partial results, retries, and permission boundaries.

### Retrieval and context failures

Retrieval quality is part of agent quality. Measure whether the needed source was retrieved, whether it was current, whether contradictory sources were present, and whether the final answer cited or reflected the evidence. A larger context window does not guarantee better context; irrelevant passages can make the decision less reliable.

The [RAG evaluation guide](/blog/rag-evaluation-guide) gives a deeper evaluation model. For a production incident, begin with one failed trace: compare retrieved chunks, rank, metadata filters, truncation, and the answer’s factual claims.

## Carry one incident through the framework

Imagine an internal operations agent asked: “Find the latest failed payment for customer 1842 and draft a message; do not refund anything.” The agent retrieves a stale payment record, calls a refund-capable tool, retries after a timeout, and drafts a confident message with the wrong date.

The visible symptom is a bad message. TRACE finds several boundaries:

1. The target included “latest” and “do not refund.”
2. The record shows the retrieval filter used an old account snapshot.
3. The assessment fails freshness and side-effect safety, even before prose quality.
4. The first divergence is retrieval freshness; tool selection is a second risk.
5. The evolution should fix the account filter, make refund tools unavailable to this task, and add a regression case.

{% highlight %}The safest fix may be a permission change, not a better prompt{% /highlight %}.

The counterfactual is useful: replay the same request with the correct current record and a read-only tool set. If the agent now drafts the correct message, the prompt was not the primary cause. If it still misstates the date, inspect the synthesis step next.

> **Decision rule:** If a failure could have caused an unauthorized side effect, treat it as a safety incident even when the final answer looks correct.

## Turn evaluations into release gates

An evaluation matters when it changes an operating decision. Define thresholds per task family rather than one universal number. For a low-risk summarizer, a small sample may support a canary. For an agent that changes billing state, correctness, authorization, and auditability may all be hard gates.

| Gate | Example question | Action when breached |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Correctness | Did the answer match the authoritative record? | Hold release or route to review |
| Safety | Did it avoid unauthorized side effects? | Block immediately and investigate |
| Reliability | Did it complete within retry and latency bounds? | Tune, degrade, or rollback |
| Economics | Did cost remain within task budget? | Review routing and context |
| Observability | Can an operator reconstruct the decision? | Do not expand traffic |

Use a fixed evaluation set for regression and a sampled production set for drift. The first tells you whether a change broke known behavior. The second tells you whether reality is moving outside the set.

## Build the feedback loop

![A production trace flows into a scorecard and branches into prompt, tool, dataset, or rollback actions before returning to the agent](/media/ai-agent-evaluation-in-production-feedback-loop.png)

The feedback loop should have an owner and a cadence. On every release, run the fixed regression set. Daily or weekly, review sampled production traces by task type. After an incident, add the trace-derived case to the regression set only after removing secrets and irrelevant personal data.

{% highlight %}A production evaluator should produce an action, not just a number{% /highlight %}.

Track changes as hypotheses: “Adding a freshness filter will reduce stale-answer failures without increasing latency beyond the task budget.” A hypothesis makes the expected trade-off explicit. It also prevents a dashboard from becoming a graveyard of unexplained scores.

### Watch for drift

Drift can appear in inputs, tools, retrieved data, user expectations, or model behavior. Segment metrics by task, model route, environment, tenant class, and prompt version. Overall averages can improve while a high-value task degrades.

Alert on changes that have an operational response. A useful alert names the segment, comparison window, threshold, and owner. “Quality down” is not an alert; “read-only billing lookup correctness below its release floor for two consecutive windows” is.

> **Field note:** Store the evaluator version with every result. Otherwise a later rubric edit can make an old score look comparable to a new one when it is not.

### Make rollback boring

Version prompts, tools, routing rules, evaluators, and retrieval configuration independently where practical. Keep the previous known-good configuration available. A rollback should reduce exposure without deleting traces needed for diagnosis.

## Make the evidence legible to the team

Evaluation only compounds when another person can understand it later. Give every run a stable link or identifier, but make the surrounding record useful even if the dashboard is unavailable. The record should answer five questions: what task was attempted, which version ran, what evidence was available, where the first divergence occurred, and what action followed.

Keep evaluator explanations tied to observable evidence. For a factuality check, identify the source passage used by the rubric. For a tool check, retain the normalized operation name and validation result rather than a raw secret-bearing payload. For a latency check, show the critical path and retry count. This makes review faster and reduces arguments about whether a score “feels right.”

### Assign ownership by failure class

Ownership prevents every incident from becoming a model-team problem. Prompt and policy regressions usually need the application or agent owner. Tool contract failures belong with the integration owner. Retrieval freshness belongs with the data or search owner. Unsafe side effects need a security or product decision, not just a better evaluator.

| Evidence pattern | Likely owner | Useful handoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Instruction ignored despite available context | Agent/application | Prompt version and replay set |
| Valid intent, invalid or stale operation | Integration | Tool contract and failing arguments |
| Correct source absent or contradicted | Data/search | Query, filters, ranking, freshness |
| Ambiguous or high-impact task | Product/security | Approval rule and escalation path |

The owner is responsible for the next experiment, not necessarily for the original cause. Record that distinction so incident review stays factual.

{% highlight %}Segmented evidence beats a flattering global average{% /highlight %}.

{% highlight %}A failed evaluation is useful when it narrows the next experiment{% /highlight %}.

{% highlight %}Keep hard safety failures separate from soft quality preferences{% /highlight %}.

{% highlight %}Review the smallest span that changed the user outcome{% /highlight %}.

Treat review time as an engineering constraint too: a trace that takes minutes to interpret will not support a healthy operating cadence.

That discipline keeps evaluation connected to shipping decisions, incident learning, and measurable user outcomes over time.

### Keep a change journal

A lightweight journal connects evaluation movement to engineering work. Record the hypothesis, changed artifact, affected task segments, baseline, result, and decision. Include negative results. A prompt change that improved summaries but increased unsupported claims is valuable evidence when it is preserved.

This journal also protects against false causality. If a model route, retrieval index, and system prompt all changed together, an improved score does not tell you which change helped. Prefer one meaningful variable per experiment unless the changes are inseparable for safety.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Decision room" title="Ship only what you can explain and reverse." body="The release candidate needs a passing regression set, known production segments, a clear owner, and a rollback path. If one is missing, hold expansion and collect the missing evidence." tone="paper" /%}

## Security and privacy boundaries

Evaluation data is often more sensitive than ordinary logs because it combines user requests, retrieved documents, tool arguments, and outputs. Redact credentials, tokens, payment details, personal identifiers, and private document content according to your retention policy. Use tenant-aware access controls and ensure evaluators cannot invoke production tools.

Do not send raw production traces to an external judge without reviewing the data path and authorization. Store the minimum evidence needed for the rubric. For high-impact actions, preserve an auditable human approval record rather than treating a judge score as authorization.

The interaction at the top of this article is a deterministic teaching scenario, not a live connection to a project. Its result does not inspect your production data and should not be used as a release decision without your own evidence.

## What changes the recommendation

The TRACE framework is intentionally conservative, but the right threshold depends on the task. A creative writing agent may prioritize user preference and style. A research agent may need citation coverage and source freshness. A customer-support agent may prioritize identity, policy accuracy, and escalation. A workflow agent that can mutate records needs the strongest tool and human-review gates.

Do not compare teams by a single “agent quality” score when their tasks have different risk. Compare the dimensions that matter to each job, and report uncertainty when the sample is small. A ten-point improvement from ten examples is a lead, not a fact about the system.

## A practical production checklist

- [ ] Define the user outcome and hard constraints for each task family.
- [ ] Capture the run and span evidence needed to reconstruct decisions.
- [ ] Evaluate correctness, safety, reliability, cost, and observability separately.
- [ ] Locate the first divergence before changing a prompt or model.
- [ ] Test one fix against a fixed regression set and sampled production traces.
- [ ] Segment results by task, route, version, environment, and risk.
- [ ] Redact sensitive data and isolate evaluators from production side effects.
- [ ] Assign an owner, release threshold, and rollback path.
- [ ] Add confirmed incidents to the regression set.

Start with one important task, not every agent behavior at once. Pick ten to twenty representative traces, label the first divergence, and make one reversible change. The goal is not a perfect score. It is a system where each score points to evidence and each evidence-backed change can be tested.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What is AI agent evaluation in production?" answer="It is the ongoing assessment of complete agent executions—including planning, retrieval, tool calls, final answers, safety, latency, and cost—using real or representative production cases." /%}
{% faq-item question="Should I fix the prompt or the tool first?" answer="Inspect the trace and fix the first meaningful divergence. If the agent had the right context but chose or formatted a tool call incorrectly, test the tool boundary; do not assume the prompt is responsible." /%}
{% faq-item question="Can an LLM judge replace human review?" answer="No. Judges can scale review for some quality dimensions, but deterministic checks and humans remain important for authorization, high-impact actions, private facts, and evaluator disagreement." /%}
{% faq-item question="How many production traces do I need?" answer="There is no universal number. Start with a representative slice large enough to expose task and failure segments, report uncertainty, and add confirmed incidents to a growing regression set." /%}
{% faq-item question="What should I monitor after an agent release?" answer="Monitor segmented correctness and safety signals alongside tool failures, latency, cost, escalation, retrieval freshness, and trace completeness. Every alert should have a threshold, owner, and response." /%}
